WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:09.250
This is how Ghulam Hassan Dar remembers his
son Adil before he disappeared last year.

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:14.520
Last month, a car packed with explosives rammed
a convoy of Indian paramilitary troops, killing

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.500
more than 40.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.730
The blast - just a few miles from here - shook
the family’s home.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.740
Ghulam Dar then received news that the suicide
bomber had been his own son.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.930
“We were shocked.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:34.660
We didn’t think he could ever do something
like this.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:37.329
Everyone started crying..

00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:38.579
Who can take pride in this?

00:00:38.579 --> 00:00:42.479
So many people have died.”

00:00:42.479 --> 00:00:47.980
The Dar family says Adil joined mass street
protests in 2016.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.199
He was injured and bedridden for months.

00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:53.149
He lost a year of college.

00:00:53.149 --> 00:00:56.010
“My son went to help a protestor who was
hit by a bullet.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:01:00.409
But, he was also shot in the leg by security
forces.

00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:06.130
He couldn’t get over the incident…
he didn’t talk to us about it but he was

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:10.200
seething with anger every time he saw the
military.”

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.700
It’s a situation Ghulam Dar says is all
too common in the region.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.299
The family lost contact with Adil last year.

00:01:19.299 --> 00:01:22.509
“We knew he had become a militant.

00:01:22.509 --> 00:01:24.579
We tried to find him.

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:26.850
But we couldn’t…

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:31.530
We can’t raise our voice here.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:34.810
The military and police make arrests and torture
people.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.179
It forces people to resist.

00:01:36.179 --> 00:01:39.610
That’s why young people want to join the
militants.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:40.990
Everybody wants to pick up a gun.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.350
It wasn’t only my child.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.500
Even well educated young people are joining
the movement…

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:52.110
“We are not terrorists, our children are
not terrorists, military personnel are not

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:53.110
terrorists.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.990
It’s politicians who are the terrorists.”

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:04.210
An armed insurgency against Indian rule first
began here in 1989.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:09.179
Though far smaller now, authorities say it’s
increasingly drawing local recruits like Adil

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:10.179
Dar.

00:02:10.179 --> 00:02:14.720
They come from villages like this one in the
southern part of the Kashmir valley, considered

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.640
a hotbed of militancy.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:23.950
Official figures show last year alone, security
forces killed more than 250 militants in the

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:25.920
region.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.190
Since the recent escalation in tensions between
India and Pakistan, the Indian army has intensified

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:33.780
its crackdown here.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.670
In recent years, the rise of the Hindu nationalist
BJP party and their tough approach to the

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:44.290
region has also stoked fears in the predominantly
Muslim Kashmir Valley.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:49.850
Many here yearn for independence from what
they see as oppressive Indian rule.

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:58.730
At the district court in Srinagar, the capital
of Indian-administered Kashmir…

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:04.380
lawyer Mir Urfi defends teenagers arrested
for protesting or providing logistical support

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.650
for alleged militants.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:12.570
Their basic rights she says aren’t protected,
fueling resentment and anger.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:17.220
“Once a person is arrested, he is to be
explained why he is arrested, he is to be

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:21.840
given access to family members, he is to be
given access to legal aid.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:23.380
All these things are denied to him.

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:30.330
He’s kept in police station for days together,
for months together without any remand, without

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:34.780
producing him before a magistrate…”When
they see these things and ask ‘why am I

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.140
not being treated like a normal person?

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:43.010
Today, the city of Srinagar has shut down
after a strike called by separatist groups.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:48.290
It’s an all too common sight in the Kashmir
Valley.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:52.780
For young people here, it’s almost impossible
to have a normal life.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:57.900
“We miss lessons when schools close down
because of the strikes.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.880
Our mothers worry we might get hit by a bullet
if we go out.”

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:10.740
“Everyone, I can assure you, everyone has
this anger that their movements are hindered

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:11.740
everywhere.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:16.479
They cannot go.+++
With India and Pakistan on high alert following

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:22.910
the recent tensions, people in Indian-controlled
Kashmir are bracing for more protests, strikes

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:28.820
and violence - further inflaming a conflict
that has already been smouldering for decades.

